            "content": [
              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Subgrids' Behavior**\n\nOkay, I've got the grid's structure nailed down: two distinct subgrids separated by a vertical color 5 line at column 10, which acts as a border between them. The full grid's dimensions are 21x10, with the output being 10x10. My focus now is on understanding how the subgrids interact, specifically, any relationships or behaviors across the dividing line, and color patterns. I'm starting by examining the example data.\n\n\n**Discovering Color Rules**\n\nOkay, I've noticed a pattern! I've gone through each coordinate pair in both left and right sides of the subgrids, and how their corresponding values interact in the output. It turns out the output color can be derived from comparing if colors from left and right grid locations are identical. Specifically, when identical colors appear in both locations, that color appears in the output. When a color appears in only the right or left, the output is different.\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
            "encrypted_content": null,
